WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.800
Now how, starting with steps one and two.

2
00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:06.825
Step one adds the security
settings the landing zone needs.

3
00:00:06.825 --> 00:00:11.713
Protect the hierarchy, as Learn's page,
Protect your resource hierarchy, shows:

4
00:00:11.713 --> 00:00:15.073
require authorization to
create management groups

5
00:00:15.073 --> 00:00:17.975
and send new subscriptions
to a default group.

6
00:00:17.975 --> 00:00:22.881
Assign the Microsoft cloud security
benchmark and the fed-ramp initiative at the

7
00:00:22.881 --> 00:00:27.138
intermediate root in do-not-enforce
mode, which, per Learn's Azure Policy

8
00:00:27.138 --> 00:00:32.188
assignment structure, evaluates compliance
without enforcing; step nine enforces.

9
00:00:32.188 --> 00:00:35.536
Send every platform resource's
logs to the workspace,

10
00:00:35.536 --> 00:00:37.438
and turn on Defender for Cloud.

11
00:00:37.438 --> 00:00:41.543
Step two builds what nothing else
can be proved complete without:

12
00:00:41.543 --> 00:00:42.988
a list of what's exposed.

13
00:00:42.988 --> 00:00:46.166
Learn recommends an
inventory of every public I P

14
00:00:46.166 --> 00:00:49.500
and its protection status,
kept with Resource Graph.

15
00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:53.356
The register adds the owner, the
D N S names and certificates,

16
00:00:53.356 --> 00:00:56.975
and whether a web application
firewall covers each one.

17
00:00:56.975 --> 00:01:00.740
It's a map of the attack surface,
so keep it inside the boundary.
